  "issue_title": "[bug] strategy.compare() budgets 3 matrices for auto RAM-vs-disk but allocates 4 (A,B,Ce,Cs) \u2014 OOM gap",
  "issue_body": "## Summary\r\n\r\n`strategy.runner.compare()` allocates **four** resident `n\u00d7n` matrices (`A`, `B`, `Ce`, `Cs`), but the `storage=\"auto\"` path still calls `should_use_disk()` which hardcodes a **three**-matrix working set (the `run()` footprint of `A+B+C`).\r\n\r\nWhen free host RAM sits in the gap where 3 matrices fit under the 50% threshold but 4 do not, `--compare` stays in RAM and can host-OOM while `run()` on the same `n` would correctly spill to memmap.\r\n\r\n## Root cause\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# strategy/storage.py \u2014 always charges 3\r\nneed = 3 * n * n * item_bytes\r\nreturn need > 0.5 * host_free\r\n\r\n# strategy/runner.py compare() \u2014 then allocates 4\r\nA = storage.generate(...)\r\nB = storage.generate(...)\r\nCe = storage.allocate(...)\r\nCs = storage.allocate(...)\r\n```\r\n\r\nConcrete gap (fp64, per-matrix = `n\u00b2\u00b78`):\r\n- `host_free` in `(6\u00b7per, 8\u00b7per]` \u2192 3 matrices \u2264 50% \u2192 RAM chosen\r\n- compare then materializes `8\u00b7per` bytes \u2192 exceeds the safety margin / can OOM\r\n\r\n## Why this is still open\r\n\r\nEarlier attempts (#198, #280) proposed a fix but were closed without merging (#280 as copycat of #198). The bug remains on `main` today \u2014 `should_use_disk` still hardcodes `3`, and `compare()` still calls it without a four-matrix budget.\r\n\r\n## Suggested fix\r\n\r\nHave `compare()` make the auto RAM-vs-disk decision against a **four**-matrix working set (without breaking `run()`'s three-matrix default). Open zone: `strategy/` only.\r\n\r\n## Reproduce (CPU)\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfrom strategy.storage import should_use_disk\r\nn, item = 100, 8\r\nper = n * n * item\r\nhost_free = 7 * per  # between 6*per and 8*per\r\nassert should_use_disk(n, item, \"auto\", host_free) is False  # run() stays RAM\r\n# compare() still uses that same helper \u2192 would also stay RAM, then allocate 4\r\n```\r\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-17T11:57:29.000Z",
